"""States and coherence: density matrix, decoherence, entanglement, qubit, wave function, coherence, macroscopic quantum phenomena, quantum biology, fluctuations, quantum numbers, superposition, Schrodinger's cat, spin."""

NARRATIVES = {
    "density_matrix": [
        ("p", r"Nuclear spins in a slightly inhomogeneous magnet are tipped by a radio-frequency pulse into the plane perpendicular to the field. The induced signal decays quickly, yet a second pulse can bring it back. Over this time each spin evolves unitarily, precessing at its own local frequency. The decay and the return belong to the ensemble, and the density matrix is the state that describes it."),
        ("p", r"If a source prepares the pure state $\ket{\psi_a}$ with probability $p_a$, every measurement probability is linear in these weights. Collecting the weights in one operator gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\rho=\sum_a p_a\ket{\psi_a}\bra{\psi_a},\qquad \rho\geq0,\qquad \operatorname{Tr}\rho=1,
\qquad p(i)=\operatorname{Tr}(\rho P_i),"""),
        ("p", r"where $P_i$ projects onto outcome $i$. Positivity and unit trace make every such probability nonnegative and their sum one. In a chosen basis the diagonal elements are populations and the off-diagonal elements are coherences; only the coherences produce interference between the basis states. Different ensembles with the same $\rho$ give identical statistics, as described for the quantum state. The purity separates a single pure preparation from a mixture, and for an isolated system $\rho$ obeys the von Neumann equation:"),
        ("eq", r"""\operatorname{Tr}\rho^2=\sum_{a,b}p_ap_b|\langle\psi_a|\psi_b\rangle|^2\leq1,
\qquad i\hbar\frac{d\rho}{dt}=[H,\rho]."""),
        ("p", r"The purity equals one only when all prepared states coincide up to a phase. In the energy basis the von Neumann equation keeps the populations fixed and rotates each coherence at its Bohr frequency, $\rho_{mn}(t)=\rho_{mn}(0)e^{-i(E_m-E_n)t/\hbar}$. Unitary evolution of the ensemble therefore preserves its purity."),
        ("p", r"Now take spin-one-half nuclei that the pulse has left polarized along $x$ with polarization $P$, so that each has $\rho_{01}=P/2$ in the $\sigma_z$ basis. In thermal nuclear magnetic resonance $P$ is small, but it only scales the signal. A nucleus whose local precession frequency differs from the reference by $\delta$ has Hamiltonian $\hbar\delta\sigma_z/2$ in the rotating frame, and its coherence becomes $\tfrac P2e^{-i\delta t}$. If $\delta$ is Gaussian distributed across the sample with standard deviation $\sigma$, the ensemble average is"),
        ("eq", r"""\rho_{01}(t)=\tfrac P2\langle e^{-i\delta t}\rangle=\tfrac P2e^{-\sigma^2t^2/2},\qquad
\operatorname{Tr}\rho^2=\tfrac12\left(1+P^2e^{-\sigma^2t^2}\right)."""),
        ("p", r"The transverse magnetization is proportional to $|\rho_{01}|$, so the signal decays on the time $\sqrt2/\sigma$. A frequency spread $\sigma/2\pi=1$ kHz gives 0.23 ms. The ensemble loses purity although every member evolves unitarily. A $\pi$ pulse about $x$ at time $\tau$ exchanges $\rho_{01}$ and $\rho_{10}$, so the phase acquired afterwards cancels the phase acquired before. At $2\tau$ every member returns to $\rho_{01}=P/2$ whatever its $\delta$. This is the Hahn spin echo."),
        ("p", r"The echo is complete only if each detuning stays constant over the interval $2\tau$. Diffusion of the spins through the field gradient, or coupling to other degrees of freedom, leaves an irreversible decay of the echo. That remaining loss is quantum decoherence: the phase has moved into correlations with an environment. A mixed state of the same kind arises whenever a subsystem is entangled with an unmeasured partner, and then $\rho_A=\operatorname{Tr}_B\rho_{AB}$. At a single time the two origins can give the same density matrix. Only a further operation, such as the echo pulse, separates them."),
    ],
    "quantum_decoherence": [
        ("p", r"Fullerene molecules sent through a grating interferometer produce clear fringes. When gas is admitted to the vacuum chamber, or when the molecules are heated before they enter, the contrast falls, although every molecule still passes the gratings. Collisions with gas molecules and thermally emitted photons leave a record of the path in the surroundings. Decoherence is the loss of interference that follows from such records."),
        ("p", r"Consider a particle in an equal superposition of two paths, $\ket L$ and $\ket R$, and an environment initially in $\ket E$. A scattering event that depends on the path leaves the environment in $\ket{E_L}$ or $\ket{E_R}$. The joint evolution is unitary. Tracing out the environment gives the reduced density matrix of the particle:"),
        ("eq", r"""\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(\ket L+\ket R)\ket E\;\longrightarrow\;
\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\left(\ket L\ket{E_L}+\ket R\ket{E_R}\right),\\
\rho_S=\tfrac12\left(\ket L\bra L+\ket R\bra R+\langle E_R|E_L\rangle\ket L\bra R
+\langle E_L|E_R\rangle\ket R\bra L\right)."""),
        ("p", r"At the screen the path amplitudes $\psi_L(x)$ and $\psi_R(x)$ overlap, and the interference term is multiplied by $\langle E_R|E_L\rangle$. For equal intensities the fringe visibility equals $|\langle E_R|E_L\rangle|$. Visibility is lost to the extent that the environment could in principle identify the path; nobody needs to examine the scattered particle."),
        ("p", r"Independent collisions multiply their overlaps. Suppose collisions occur at random with mean rate $\Gamma$, and each leaves a real overlap $\chi$ between zero and one. Averaging $\chi^N$ over the Poisson distribution of the number $N$ of collisions in time $t$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""V(t)=\sum_{N=0}^\infty e^{-\Gamma t}\frac{(\Gamma t)^N}{N!}\,\chi^N=e^{-\Gamma(1-\chi)t}."""),
        ("p", r"The decoherence rate $\Gamma(1-\chi)$ depends on how well one collision resolves the path. A scattered wave whose wavelength $\lambda$ greatly exceeds the path separation $d$ gives $\chi$ close to one, with $1-\chi$ growing as $(d/\lambda)^2$. When $d\gg\lambda$, a single event suffices and the rate saturates at $\Gamma$. Since $\Gamma$ is proportional to the gas pressure, the visibility falls exponentially with pressure. This dependence was measured for $\mathrm{C}_{70}$ molecules in a Talbot--Lau interferometer. Heated molecules emit more thermal photons and lose visibility in the same way."),
        ("p", r"Over times long compared with one collision, the same decay follows from a Lindblad master equation with a dephasing operator. With $\sigma_z=\ket L\bra L-\ket R\bra R$ and rate $\gamma=\Gamma(1-\chi)$,"),
        ("eq", r"""\frac{d\rho}{dt}=-\frac{i}{\hbar}[H,\rho]+\frac{\gamma}{2}\left(\sigma_z\rho\sigma_z-\rho\right),
\qquad \frac{d\rho_{LR}}{dt}\Big|_{\rm env}=-\gamma\rho_{LR}."""),
        ("p", r"The populations of the two paths are unchanged; only their coherence decays. Coherence is lost in the basis in which the interaction is diagonal, here position. Decoherence therefore explains why records form in that basis. It does not determine on which path a given molecule is found; that question belongs to the measurement problem."),
        ("p", r"The master equation assumes that each scattered particle leaves and never returns, so that records made at different times are independent. A small environment violates this assumption. For one environmental spin, initially up along its $z$ axis and coupled through $\hbar g\sigma_z\otimes\sigma_x^E$, the overlap is $\cos2gt$, and the visibility $|\cos2gt|$ returns to one at $t=\pi/2g$. Such revivals, and the restored fringes of the quantum eraser experiment, show that the phase information has moved into system-environment correlations rather than being destroyed. A Markovian master equation for the particle alone cannot describe them."),
    ],
    "quantum_entanglement": [
        ("p", r"Polarization-entangled photon pairs from parametric down-conversion each appear unpolarized when examined alone: a polarizer at any angle passes half of them. When both photons of a pair meet linear polarizers set to a common angle, whichever angle is chosen, their results agree. Pairs with definite and correlated polarizations can reproduce the agreement along one axis, but not along two axes such as $0^\circ$ and $45^\circ$. A joint state that does not factor into states of the two photons accounts for both observations."),
        ("p", r"For subsystems $A$ and $B$ the joint state space is the tensor product $\mathcal H_A\otimes\mathcal H_B$. Expand a pure state as $\ket\Psi=\sum_{ij}c_{ij}\ket{i}\ket{j}$ in product bases. The singular-value decomposition of the coefficient matrix $c$ rewrites it as a single sum, the Schmidt form:"),
        ("eq", r"""\ket\Psi=\sum_k\sqrt{\lambda_k}\,\ket{k_A}\ket{k_B},\qquad \sum_k\lambda_k=1,\qquad
\rho_A=\operatorname{Tr}_B\ket\Psi\bra\Psi=\sum_k\lambda_k\ket{k_A}\bra{k_A}."""),
        ("p", r"The state is a product exactly when only one Schmidt coefficient is nonzero. Otherwise each subsystem is in a mixed state although the pair is pure. Both reduced states have the eigenvalues $\lambda_k$, so the entanglement entropy is the same on either side:"),
        ("eq", r"""S_A=-\operatorname{Tr}\rho_A\log_2\rho_A=-\sum_k\lambda_k\log_2\lambda_k=S_B."""),
        ("p", r"Local unitary operations change the Schmidt vectors but not the coefficients. Local operations assisted by classical communication cannot increase this entropy on average. For $\cos\theta\ket{HH}+\sin\theta\ket{VV}$ the coefficients are $\cos^2\theta$ and $\sin^2\theta$. The choice $\theta=\pi/4$ gives one bit, the largest value for two-level systems, and $\theta=\pi/8$ gives 0.60 bit."),
        ("p", r"Noise turns a pure pair into a mixed state, and then the failure of one product decomposition proves nothing. A mixed state is separable when it can be written as $\rho=\sum_kp_k\rho_k^A\otimes\rho_k^B$ with probabilities $p_k$, and entangled otherwise. Transposing only the $B$ factor maps every separable state to a positive operator. The Werner state mixes the singlet $\ket{\Psi^-}$ with white noise, and its partial transpose has the eigenvalues"),
        ("eq", r"""\rho_W=p\ket{\Psi^-}\bra{\Psi^-}+(1-p)\frac{I}{4},\qquad
\operatorname{spec}\rho_W^{T_B}=\left\{\tfrac{1+p}{4},\tfrac{1+p}{4},\tfrac{1+p}{4},\tfrac{1-3p}{4}\right\}."""),
        ("p", r"A negative eigenvalue appears for $p>1/3$, and for two qubits the criterion of Peres and the Horodeckis is also sufficient. The largest Clauser--Horne--Shimony--Holt combination for this state is $2\sqrt2\,p$, so the inequality is violated only for $p>1/\sqrt2\approx0.71$. For $1/3<p\leq1/\sqrt2$ the pairs are entangled, yet their correlations violate no such inequality. Werner constructed a local hidden-variable model for all projective measurements when $p\leq1/2$. Entanglement and Bell nonlocality are therefore different properties; Bell's theorem concerns the second."),
        ("p", r"Entanglement is defined relative to a stated division into subsystems. A single photon sent through a balanced beam splitter is entangled between the two output modes, while in the input modes it is a product of one photon and vacuum. The relevant division is the one matched to the available operations, such as two separate laboratories. With that division fixed, a shared Bell pair is the resource consumed in quantum teleportation."),
    ],
    "qubit": [
        ("p", r"A trapped ion, the polarization of a photon and the lowest two levels of a superconducting circuit each provide two states that can be prepared, rotated and distinguished. Whether such a pair works as a qubit depends on how strictly the dynamics stays within the two states. It also depends on how long their relative phase survives. Within the chosen pair a pure state is"),
        ("eq", r"""\ket\psi=\alpha\ket0+\beta\ket1,\qquad |\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2=1,\qquad
p(0)=|\langle0|\psi\rangle|^2=|\alpha|^2,\qquad p(1)=|\beta|^2."""),
        ("p", r"A global phase has no observable effect, so a pure state is fixed by two real numbers: the population ratio and the relative phase. These coordinates form the Bloch sphere described for the quantum state. A measurement in the basis $\{\ket0,\ket1\}$ returns one of two outcomes and removes the relative phase. The continuous amplitudes therefore cannot be read off in one shot. If a bit is encoded in one of two pure states with equal prior probability, any measurement errs with probability at least the Helstrom bound:"),
        ("eq", r"""P_{\rm err}=\frac12\left(1-\sqrt{1-|\langle\psi_0|\psi_1\rangle|^2}\right)."""),
        ("p", r"Orthogonal states give zero error, while an overlap $|\langle\psi_0|\psi_1\rangle|=\cos(\pi/8)$ gives 0.31. More generally, Holevo's theorem limits the classical information retrievable from one qubit to one bit, despite the continuum of states. A previously shared entangled pair raises this to two bits in superdense coding."),
        ("p", r"A superconducting transmon shows what restricting a system to two levels involves. It is a weakly anharmonic oscillator whose lowest transition frequency $\omega_{01}/2\pi$ typically lies between 4 and 6 GHz. The next transition is lower by an anharmonicity $|\eta|/2\pi$ of about 200 to 300 MHz. A control pulse at $\omega_{01}$ also drives the transition from $\ket1$ to $\ket2$, detuned by $\eta$. By the off-resonant Rabi formula of the two-state quantum system, this leakage stays small only when the drive strength and the pulse bandwidth are well below $|\eta|$. Single-qubit gates therefore last tens of nanoseconds."),
        ("p", r"Relaxation sets the second limit. Energy decay from $\ket1$ to $\ket0$ at rate $1/T_1$, together with additional pure dephasing at rate $\Gamma_\phi$, gives in the Markovian regime at zero temperature"),
        ("eq", r"""\rho_{11}(t)=\rho_{11}(0)e^{-t/T_1},\qquad |\rho_{01}(t)|=|\rho_{01}(0)|e^{-t/T_2},
\qquad \frac{1}{T_2}=\frac{1}{2T_1}+\Gamma_\phi."""),
        ("p", r"The factor one half follows from the decay itself. The amplitude of $\ket1$ falls as $e^{-t/2T_1}$, and the coherence $\rho_{01}$ is proportional to that amplitude. Hence $T_2\leq2T_1$, with equality when energy decay is the only process. An echo experiment measures $T_2$ from the decay of the recovered phase coherence. Comparing it with $T_1$ from a population decay separates loss of energy from loss of phase. Quantum error correction encodes one logical qubit in several physical ones so that errors of both kinds can be detected and reversed."),
    ],
    "wave_function": [
        ("p", r"Electrons accelerated through 54 volts and directed onto a nickel crystal leave its surface preferentially at about $50^\circ$ from the incident beam. Davisson and Germer observed this peak in 1927. Point particles scattered by the surface atoms give no reason for such a sharp preferred angle, whereas a wave of definite wavelength does. The wave function supplies that wave and also the probability of detecting each electron at a given place."),
        ("p", r"For one spinless particle, the state $\ket\psi$ is represented in the position basis by the complex function $\psi(\mathbf x)=\langle\mathbf x|\psi\rangle$. The Born rule gives the probability that a detector covering the region $\Delta$ responds:"),
        ("eq", r"""\Pr(\mathbf x\in\Delta)=\int_\Delta|\psi(\mathbf x)|^2d^3x,\qquad
\int|\psi|^2d^3x=1,\qquad \psi\sim e^{i\alpha}\psi."""),
        ("p", r"A constant overall phase $e^{i\alpha}$ changes no probability, so $\psi$ and $e^{i\alpha}\psi$ describe the same state. Phase differences between regions are physical. The Schrödinger equation $i\hbar\partial_t\psi=-(\hbar^2/2m)\nabla^2\psi+V\psi$ conserves probability locally, and the current through a surface depends on the phase gradient. Writing $\psi=R\,e^{iS/\hbar}$ with real $R$ and $S$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\partial_t|\psi|^2+\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0,\qquad
\mathbf j=\frac{\hbar}{m}\operatorname{Im}(\psi^*\nabla\psi)=\frac{R^2\,\nabla S}{m}."""),
        ("p", r"The phase gradient divided by the mass acts as a local velocity. A plane wave $e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x}$ carries a uniform current, is an eigenfunction of the momentum operator $-i\hbar\nabla$ with eigenvalue $\hbar\mathbf k$, and has wavelength $\lambda=2\pi/k=h/p$. For an electron of kinetic energy $E$ this gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\lambda=\frac{h}{\sqrt{2m_eE}}=0.167\ \mathrm{nm}\ \ (E=54\ \mathrm{eV}),\qquad
D\sin\theta=\lambda."""),
        ("p", r"Waves scattered from parallel rows of surface atoms with spacing $D$ add in phase when their path difference $D\sin\theta$ equals one wavelength. With $D=0.215$ nm for nickel, the first-order angle is $50.9^\circ$, close to the observed peak. The electron speed is about 1.5 percent of the speed of light, so the nonrelativistic relation suffices. The angle arises from the relative phases of the waves from different atoms; adding the probabilities $|\psi|^2$ contributed by each atom would give no peak."),
        ("p", r"For several particles the wave function lives on configuration space. Two particles require $\psi(\mathbf x_1,\mathbf x_2)$, a function of six coordinates, not two waves in ordinary space. Because $\psi$ need not factor into a function of $\mathbf x_1$ times one of $\mathbf x_2$, quantum entanglement is possible. Spin adds discrete components, so a particle of spin $s$ has a wave function with $2s+1$ components. For identical particles $\psi$ must be symmetric or antisymmetric under exchange of their coordinates. The wave function is not recorded by a single detector. Its modulus follows from position statistics of identically prepared particles, and its phase from interference or from momentum statistics. The Fourier transform gives the equivalent momentum-space wave function, and a statistical preparation requires a density matrix."),
    ],
    "coherence_physics": [
        ("p", r"A Michelson interferometer illuminated by a filtered lamp shows fringes only while its two arms have nearly equal lengths. With a single-frequency laser, fringes persist over path differences of many metres. The fringe contrast as a function of the delay between the arms is a measurable property of the light, its first-order coherence."),
        ("p", r"Let $E(t)$ be the complex field amplitude at the detector, let one arm delay it by $\tau$, and let a balanced beam splitter recombine the two parts. Averaged over the fluctuations of a stationary source of intensity $I_0$, the detected intensity is"),
        ("eq", r"""I(\tau)=\frac{I_0}{2}\left[1+\operatorname{Re}g^{(1)}(\tau)\right],\qquad
g^{(1)}(\tau)=\frac{\langle E^*(t)E(t+\tau)\rangle}{\langle|E(t)|^2\rangle}."""),
        ("p", r"As $\tau$ changes over one optical period, $I$ oscillates between maxima and minima with visibility $V=|g^{(1)}(\tau)|$. By the Wiener--Khinchin theorem, $g^{(1)}$ is the Fourier transform of the normalized power spectrum $S(\omega)$. A Lorentzian line centred at $\omega_0$ with full width $\Gamma$ at half maximum gives"),
        ("eq", r"""g^{(1)}(\tau)=\int S(\omega)\,e^{-i\omega\tau}d\omega=e^{-i\omega_0\tau-\Gamma|\tau|/2}."""),
        ("p", r"Fringes therefore fade once the delay exceeds a time of order $1/\Gamma$. In terms of path difference, light of wavelength $\lambda$ and spectral width $\Delta\lambda$ has a coherence length of order $\lambda^2/\Delta\lambda$. A filter 1 nm wide at 600 nm gives about 0.36 mm, and a laser line 1 MHz wide gives about 300 m. The same quantity describes one photon in a two-path interferometer. There the visibility equals $2|\rho_{LR}|$, twice the coherence between the paths in the density matrix, and quantum decoherence reduces it."),
        ("p", r"Intensity correlations distinguish sources with identical spectra. Hanbury Brown and Twiss correlated the photocurrents of two detectors and found correlated intensity fluctuations in thermal light. Chaotic light is the sum of fields from very many independent emitters, so the field has Gaussian statistics. Its fourth moment then factorizes into pairs of second moments, which gives the Siegert relation:"),
        ("eq", r"""g^{(2)}(\tau)=\frac{\langle I(t)I(t+\tau)\rangle}{\langle I\rangle^2}=1+|g^{(1)}(\tau)|^2,
\qquad g^{(2)}(0)=\frac{\langle n(n-1)\rangle}{\langle n\rangle^2}=2."""),
        ("p", r"At zero delay the intensity correlation is twice the uncorrelated value; in photon terms, thermal light arrives in bunches. The same value 2 follows from the geometric distribution of photon numbers $n$ in one thermal mode. The excess lasts about one coherence time, and a detector much slower than that averages most of it away. Hanbury Brown and Twiss nevertheless measured how the excess declined with the separation of two telescopes, and obtained the angular diameter of Sirius in 1956."),
        ("p", r"A classical intensity satisfies $\langle I^2\rangle\geq\langle I\rangle^2$, which forces $g^{(2)}(0)\geq1$, and an ideal laser beam gives exactly one. Values below one, which no fluctuating classical wave can produce, come from single-photon sources treated under photon. First-order coherence cannot separate these cases, because sources with the same spectrum give the same fringes."),
    ],
    "macroscopic_quantum_phenomena": [
        ("p", r"A superconducting ring cooled through its transition in a weak magnetic field traps the flux threading it. The trapped flux takes only discrete values, separated by about $2\times10^{-15}$ Wb, a step fixed by Planck's constant and the electron charge. The ring can be millimetres across, yet its flux, a macroscopic electrical variable, is quantized."),
        ("p", r"Below the transition temperature, electrons bound in Cooper pairs share one wave function $\psi=\sqrt{n_s}\,e^{i\theta}$, where $n_s$ is the pair density and $\theta$ a phase common to all pairs. A pair has charge $q=-2e$ and mass $m^*=2m_e$. Its current density in a vector potential $\mathbf A$ depends on the phase gradient. Deep inside a ring thicker than the magnetic penetration depth the current vanishes. Integrating around such a path, and requiring $\psi$ to be single-valued, gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathbf j_s=\frac{qn_s}{m^*}\left(\hbar\nabla\theta-q\mathbf A\right)=0
\;\Rightarrow\;2\pi\hbar n=q\oint\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf l,\\
\Phi=n\,\frac{h}{2e}=n\Phi_0,\qquad \Phi_0=2.07\times10^{-15}\ \mathrm{Wb}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $n$ is an integer and the sign of the charge is absorbed into it. Deaver and Fairbank, and independently Doll and Näbauer, measured this quantization in 1961 in small superconducting cylinders. The step $h/2e$, not $h/e$, showed that the condensate carries charge in pairs."),
        ("p", r"Two superconductors joined through a thin insulating barrier have a phase difference $\varphi$ across it. Pairs tunnel through the barrier, and the Josephson relations connect the current $I$ and the voltage $V$ to that phase:"),
        ("eq", r"""I=I_c\sin\varphi,\qquad \frac{d\varphi}{dt}=\frac{2eV}{\hbar}."""),
        ("p", r"A constant voltage makes the current oscillate at $2eV/h$, which is 483.6 GHz per millivolt. Microwaves of frequency $f$ produce current steps at the voltages $nhf/2e$. Voltage standards use arrays of such junctions, because the step voltages depend only on $f$ and fundamental constants."),
        ("p", r"The phase difference is itself a dynamical variable. For a junction of capacitance $C$ biased by a current $I$, it moves like a particle of mass $C(\hbar/2e)^2$ in a tilted washboard potential:"),
        ("eq", r"""U(\varphi)=-E_J\cos\varphi-\frac{\hbar I}{2e}\varphi,\qquad E_J=\frac{\hbar I_c}{2e},\\
\omega_p=\sqrt{\frac{2eI_c}{\hbar C}}\left(1-\frac{I^2}{I_c^2}\right)^{1/4}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $\omega_p$ is the frequency of small oscillations in a well. Near $I_c$ each well becomes shallow, and escape from it switches the junction to a finite voltage. At high temperature escape is thermally activated. Below a crossover temperature near $\hbar\omega_p/2\pi k_B$, about 76 mK for $\omega_p/2\pi=10$ GHz, the escape rate stops depending on temperature because the phase tunnels through the barrier. Dissipation reduces the tunnelling rate, as Caldeira and Leggett showed, so the junction must be weakly damped."),
        ("p", r"Devoret, Martinis and Clarke observed this macroscopic tunnelling in 1985, and microwave spectroscopy resolved quantized energy levels of the phase in the well. This work, recognized by the 2025 Nobel Prize in Physics, underlies superconducting qubits. These effects are established, and their macroscopic character lies in one collective coordinate of very many pairs. Superpositions of macroscopically distinct states, such as opposite currents circulating in a ring, raise a further question. Several measures of macroscopicity have been proposed, and they rank such experiments differently, which leaves the comparison with Schrödinger's cat open."),
    ],
    "quantum_biology": [
        ("p", r"Migratory birds orient in the geomagnetic field, whose strength of about 50 microtesla changes the energy of an electron spin by roughly $2\times10^{-7}$ of the thermal energy at body temperature. A thermal population effect of that size is negligible. A chemical reaction controlled by the spin dynamics of a short-lived radical pair can still respond to such a field. This radical-pair mechanism is the most developed proposal for a magnetic sense, and its central calculation is a singlet yield."),
        ("p", r"Light absorption moves an electron between two molecules and creates two radicals, each with one unpaired electron. The pair starts in the singlet state, with total electron spin zero. In the simplest kinetic model the pair reacts at rate $k$ whatever its spin state, singlet pairs to one product and triplet pairs to another. Between creation and reaction the electron spins evolve under the Zeeman interaction and hyperfine couplings to nuclear spins. With one spin-one-half nucleus $\mathbf I$ on radical 1, isotropic coupling $a$ and equal g-factors,"),
        ("eq", r"""\frac{H}{\hbar}=\omega(S_{1z}+S_{2z})+a\,\mathbf S_1\cdot\mathbf I,\qquad \omega=\frac{g\mu_BB}{\hbar},
\qquad \Phi_S=k\int_0^\infty e^{-kt}P_S(t)\,dt."""),
        ("p", r"Here $P_S(t)$ is the singlet probability and $\Phi_S$ the singlet yield; the nuclear spin starts unpolarized. In zero field, the hyperfine term splits electron 1 and the nucleus into total spin one and zero, separated by $a$, and the singlet probability oscillates at that frequency. In a strong field, $\omega\gg a$, the triplet states with $m=\pm1$ are shifted away. Only the triplet with $m=0$ then mixes with the singlet, at frequency $a/2$. Integrating against the survival factor gives the two yields:"),
        ("eq", r"""P_S^{(0)}(t)=\tfrac58+\tfrac38\cos at,\qquad P_S^{(\infty)}(t)=\tfrac12+\tfrac12\cos\tfrac{at}{2},\\
\Phi_S^{(0)}=\tfrac58+\tfrac38\,\frac{k^2}{k^2+a^2},\qquad
\Phi_S^{(\infty)}=\tfrac12+\tfrac12\,\frac{k^2}{k^2+a^2/4}."""),
        ("p", r"For fast reaction, $k\gg a$, both yields approach one and the field has no effect, because the spins have no time to evolve. For slow reaction the yield falls from 5/8 to 1/2. This 20 percent change is produced by a field whose energy is negligible compared with $k_BT$. A field weaker than $a$ acts through the Zeeman splitting of nearly degenerate levels. In this model fields of $0.05a$ to $0.2a$ lower the slow-reaction yield to about 0.4. That response requires the pair to live, and its spins to stay coherent, longer than the inverse Larmor frequency, 0.11 microseconds at 50 microtesla."),
        ("p", r"Sensing the direction of the field requires anisotropic hyperfine couplings, so that the yield depends on the angle between the field and the molecule. Cryptochrome proteins in the retina form flavin-tryptophan radical pairs and are the leading candidates. Magnetic field effects on radical-pair reactions are established in spin chemistry. Effects on isolated cryptochromes have been measured in fields of a few millitesla. Whether the avian compass works in this way at 50 microtesla in vivo, and how spin coherence survives for a microsecond in a protein, remain open."),
        ("p", r"Other parts of quantum biology show the same division between an established microscopic step and an open biological role. Proton and hydride transfer in some enzymes shows kinetic isotope effects far larger than transfer over the barrier allows, which quantum tunnelling explains. Long-lived oscillations in two-dimensional spectra of light-harvesting complexes were initially attributed to electronic coherence. They are now largely assigned to vibrational or vibronic motion, and their functional role is not established. In each case quantum decoherence sets how long a coherent step can last, and the biological question is whether it changes a rate or yield that matters to the organism."),
    ],
    "quantum_fluctuation": [
        ("p", r"Two uncharged, parallel metal plates a micrometre apart in vacuum attract each other with a pressure of about one millipascal. Casimir predicted this force in 1948. It has been measured since 1997 between a sphere and a plate and between parallel plates. The force follows from the zero-point fluctuations of the electromagnetic field between the plates."),
        ("p", r"Each mode of the field is a harmonic oscillator of angular frequency $\omega$. As for the quantum harmonic oscillator, its ground state has energy $\hbar\omega/2$ and nonzero variances of both field quadratures: the mean field vanishes, but its square does not. Conducting plates change the allowed modes and therefore the sum of zero-point energies. The logic is clearest for a scalar field in one dimension between mirrors a distance $d$ apart, where the mode frequencies are $\omega_n=n\pi c/d$:"),
        ("eq", r"""E_0(d)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{\hbar\omega_n}{2}=\frac{\pi\hbar c}{2d}\sum_{n=1}^\infty n."""),
        ("p", r"The sum diverges, but real mirrors become transparent at high frequency. A factor $e^{-\epsilon\omega_n}$, with $\epsilon$ of order the inverse plasma frequency of the metal, represents that transparency. The sum is then elementary, $\sum_nne^{-\lambda n}=\lambda^{-2}-1/12+O(\lambda^2)$ with $\lambda=\epsilon\pi c/d$, and gives"),
        ("eq", r"""E_0(d)=\frac{\hbar d}{2\pi c\,\epsilon^2}-\frac{\pi\hbar c}{24d}+O(\epsilon^2)."""),
        ("p", r"The first term is proportional to $d$, with the same energy per length as in free space. In a long cavity divided by a movable mirror, the linear terms from the two sides add to a constant and exert no net force. The remaining term does not depend on the cutoff and gives an attraction of magnitude $\pi\hbar c/24d^2$. For the electromagnetic field between perfectly conducting plates of area $A$, the same steps with two polarizations and all transverse wavevectors give"),
        ("eq", r"""\frac{E}{A}=-\frac{\pi^2\hbar c}{720\,d^3},\qquad \frac{F}{A}=-\frac{\pi^2\hbar c}{240\,d^4}."""),
        ("p", r"At $d=1$ micrometre the pressure is 1.3 mPa, and halving the separation multiplies it by 16. This steep rise makes the force relevant to the sticking of nearby parts in micromachined devices. The ideal-conductor formula requires the separation to exceed the plasma wavelength of the metal, about 0.14 micrometre for gold. At smaller separations finite conductivity weakens the force, and Lifshitz theory with measured dielectric functions replaces the formula. At room temperature thermal photons add a correction once $d$ approaches $\hbar c/k_BT\approx7.6$ micrometres."),
        ("p", r"The calculation does not show that empty space has a measurable absolute energy density. The force depends on how the plates respond to the field, and it vanishes as their coupling to the field goes to zero. It can be derived equally as the retarded van der Waals attraction between fluctuating charges in the two plates, without reference to vacuum energy. What is established is how the zero-point energy changes with the boundary conditions. The coupling of bound electrons to the same field fluctuations contributes to the Lamb shift treated by quantum electrodynamics. The absolute vacuum energy and its relation to the cosmological constant remain open."),
    ],
    "quantum_number": [
        ("p", r"The visible lines of hydrogen, such as the red Balmer line near 656 nm, are labelled by integers. With higher resolution each line splits into components, and a magnetic field splits them further. Quantum numbers name the stationary states between which these transitions occur. Which integers are good labels depends on which interactions the Hamiltonian contains."),
        ("p", r"A quantum number labels an eigenvalue of an observable that commutes with the Hamiltonian, so that its value is conserved by the evolution. Several such observables that also commute with one another can be diagonalized together. For the nonrelativistic hydrogen atom with a Coulomb potential, $H$, $L^2$, $L_z$ and $S_z$ form such a set:"),
        ("eq", r"""[H,L^2]=[H,L_z]=[L^2,L_z]=0,\qquad H\ket{n\,l\,m_l\,m_s}=E_n\ket{n\,l\,m_l\,m_s},\\
E_n=-\frac{R_y}{n^2},\qquad R_y=\frac{m_ee^4}{2(4\pi\varepsilon_0)^2\hbar^2}=13.6\ \mathrm{eV}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $n=1,2,\ldots$; for each $n$ the orbital number $l$ runs from $0$ to $n-1$, $m_l$ from $-l$ to $l$, and $m_s=\pm1/2$. The ranges of $l$ and $m_l$ follow from the algebra of the angular momentum operator, and the bound $l\leq n-1$ from the radial equation. The energy depends on $n$ alone, so the number of states in a level is"),
        ("eq", r"""g_n=\sum_{l=0}^{n-1}2(2l+1)=2n^2."""),
        ("p", r"The counts 2, 8, 18 and 32 match the shell capacities of the periodic table. In atoms with many electrons, screening by inner electrons removes the degeneracy in $l$, and the filling order departs from the shell order. The Balmer line connects $n=3$ to $n=2$, with $1/\lambda=(R_y/hc)(1/4-1/9)$ up to a small reduced-mass correction. Electric-dipole transitions obey $\Delta l=\pm1$ and $\Delta m_l=0,\pm1$, so the labels also decide which lines appear."),
        ("p", r"Spin-orbit coupling adds a term proportional to $\mathbf L\cdot\mathbf S=(J^2-L^2-S^2)/2$, which conserves the total angular momentum $\mathbf J=\mathbf L+\mathbf S$ but not $m_l$ or $m_s$ separately. The good quantum numbers become $n$, $l$, $j=l\pm1/2$ and $m_j$, and the resulting fine-structure splitting is worked out under the Dirac equation."),
        ("p", r"A magnetic field $B$ whose Zeeman energies are small compared with that splitting shifts each level according to $m_j$. The interaction $\mu_BB(L_z+g_sS_z)/\hbar$ is evaluated within a level of fixed $j$. The spin moment carries $g_s\approx2$ and the orbital moment carries one, so projecting both onto $\mathbf J$ gives the Landé factor:"),
        ("eq", r"""\Delta E=g_J\mu_BBm_j,\qquad g_J=1+\frac{j(j+1)+s(s+1)-l(l+1)}{2j(j+1)},\\
g_J(s_{1/2})=2,\qquad g_J(p_{1/2})=\tfrac23,\qquad g_J(p_{3/2})=\tfrac43."""),
        ("p", r"An electric-dipole photon carries one unit of angular momentum, so only transitions with $\Delta m_j=0,\pm1$ appear. Their photon energies are shifted by $\mu_BB(g'm_j'-gm_j)$, where primes mark the upper level. For the sodium D lines, from $3p_{1/2}$ and $3p_{3/2}$ to $3s_{1/2}$, the shifts in units of $\mu_BB$ are"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathrm{D}_1:\ \pm\tfrac23,\ \pm\tfrac43\qquad\qquad \mathrm{D}_2:\ \pm\tfrac13,\ \pm1,\ \pm\tfrac53."""),
        ("p", r"D1 therefore splits into four components and D2 into six. In a field of 1 T the outer D2 components lie 23 GHz from the line centre, far beyond the Doppler width of about 1.5 GHz in a sodium lamp. The field is still weak in the required sense, because the fine-structure splitting of sodium is 515 GHz. Hyperfine shifts from the nuclear spin are smaller and are neglected here. These patterns, the anomalous Zeeman effect, could be explained only after electron spin with $g_s\approx2$ was introduced. When both levels have zero total spin, as for the red cadmium line at 643.8 nm, $g_J=1$ in both levels. The components then merge into the normal triplet at $0$ and $\pm\mu_BB$. Viewed along the field, the $\Delta m_j=0$ component is absent, a direct test of the selection rule. A strong field decouples $\mathbf L$ from $\mathbf S$ and restores $m_l$ and $m_s$ as approximate labels."),
        ("p", r"Each quantum number thus records a symmetry of the Hamiltonian at a stated level of approximation. Rotational invariance conserves $j$ and $m_j$, and reflection symmetry gives the parity $(-1)^l$. The extra degeneracy in $l$ of the pure Coulomb problem comes from a further conserved quantity, the Laplace--Runge--Lenz vector. An interaction that breaks a symmetry splits a level into components carrying the new labels, and spectroscopy measures those splittings."),
    ],
    "quantum_superposition": [
        ("p", r"The Super-Kamiokande detector in Japan counts muon neutrinos produced by cosmic rays in the atmosphere. In 1998 the collaboration reported a deficit of muon neutrinos of a few GeV arriving from below, after crossing up to 13000 km of the Earth. They were about half as numerous as those arriving from above, which travel some 15 km. Electron neutrinos showed no such deficit. The missing muon neutrinos had changed flavour in flight. That change requires each neutrino to travel as a superposition of states with different masses."),
        ("p", r"The Schrödinger equation is linear. If $\ket a$ and $\ket b$ are possible states, so is every normalized combination of them, and each component evolves with its own phase. For an equal superposition with relative phase $\varphi$, a measurement that projects onto $\ket\pm=(\ket a\pm\ket b)/\sqrt2$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\ket\psi=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\left(\ket a+e^{i\varphi}\ket b\right),\qquad
P_\pm=|\langle\pm|\psi\rangle|^2=\frac12\left(1\pm\cos\varphi\right)."""),
        ("p", r"In a two-path interferometer $\ket a$ and $\ket b$ are the paths and the two outputs realize $\ket\pm$. A mixture with equal probability of either state gives a different prediction:"),
        ("eq", r"""\rho_{\rm mix}=\frac12\left(\ket a\bra a+\ket b\bra b\right),\qquad
P_\pm=\operatorname{Tr}\left(\rho_{\rm mix}\ket\pm\bra\pm\right)=\frac12."""),
        ("p", r"Both descriptions assign probability one half to each of $\ket a$ and $\ket b$, so a measurement in that basis cannot tell them apart. Only the superposition responds to $\varphi$. The term $\cos\varphi$ comes from the off-diagonal elements of the density matrix and is the measurable signature of the superposition. Whether a state counts as a superposition depends on the basis: $\ket+$ is a superposition of $\ket a$ and $\ket b$ but an eigenstate of the output measurement."),
        ("p", r"A neutrino is created by the weak interaction in a flavour state, here $\nu_\mu$ together with a muon. In a two-flavour model the flavour states are rotations of the mass eigenstates $\nu_2$ and $\nu_3$ by a mixing angle $\theta$:"),
        ("eq", r"""\ket{\nu_\mu}=\cos\theta\ket{\nu_2}+\sin\theta\ket{\nu_3},\qquad
\ket{\nu_\tau}=-\sin\theta\ket{\nu_2}+\cos\theta\ket{\nu_3}."""),
        ("p", r"In natural units a mass state of momentum $p\gg m_i$ has energy $E_i\approx p+m_i^2/2E$. After a distance $L\approx t$ the two components have therefore acquired the relative phase $\Delta m^2L/2E$, with $\Delta m^2=m_3^2-m_2^2$. Projecting the evolved state onto $\ket{\nu_\tau}$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""P(\nu_\mu\to\nu_\tau)=\sin^22\theta\,\sin^2\frac{\Delta m^2L}{4E}
=\sin^22\theta\,\sin^2\!\left(1.27\,\frac{\Delta m^2[\mathrm{eV}^2]\,L[\mathrm{km}]}{E[\mathrm{GeV}]}\right)."""),
        ("p", r"The conversion probability returns to zero after the oscillation length $4\pi E/\Delta m^2$. For $\Delta m^2=2.5\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{eV}^2$ and $E=1$ GeV this is about 990 km, with the first maximum near 500 km. Downward neutrinos, after 15 km, have converted with probability below one percent. Upward neutrinos have passed through several oscillation lengths. Averaged over their spread of energies and path lengths, the second factor gives one half. With nearly maximal mixing, $\sin^22\theta\approx1$, about half of them arrive as $\nu_\tau$, which the detector did not record as muon neutrinos."),
        ("p", r"Suppose instead that the source emitted an incoherent mixture, $\nu_2$ with probability $\cos^2\theta$ and $\nu_3$ with probability $\sin^2\theta$. Each mass state is stationary and keeps its flavour content, so the conversion probability is $\cos^2\theta\sin^2\theta+\sin^2\theta\cos^2\theta=\tfrac12\sin^22\theta$ at every distance. That value equals the average of the oscillating formula but does not depend on $L$. It would remove half of the downward muon neutrinos as well, which the data exclude. Only the coherent superposition starts as a pure $\nu_\mu$ and develops the conversion with distance."),
        ("p", r"The coherent description requires the wave packets of the two mass components to overlap at the detector. Over very long distances their different group velocities separate them, and the oscillating term averages away, as described under quantum decoherence. The two-flavour formula neglects the third neutrino; matter in the Earth changes electron-neutrino oscillations but hardly affects the conversion of $\nu_\mu$ into $\nu_\tau$. Superpositions of states with different electric charge are never observed, an example of a superselection rule. Superpositions of macroscopically distinct states are the subject of Schrödinger's cat."),
    ],
    "schr_dinger_s_cat": [
        ("p", r"In 1935 Schrödinger imagined a cat sealed in a chamber with a radioactive atom, a Geiger counter and a flask of poison. If the atom decays, the counter releases a hammer that breaks the flask. After the time at which the decay probability is one half, linear evolution of the whole system gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\ket\Psi=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\left(\ket{u}\ket{\rm alive}+\ket{d}\ket{\rm dead}\right),"""),
        ("p", r"where $\ket u$ and $\ket d$ are the undecayed and decayed atom. Schrödinger presented the state as an absurd consequence of applying the wave function to everyday objects. Two physical questions remain: which observation would distinguish this state from a mixture of living and dead cats, and why no such observation succeeds."),
        ("p", r"Only interference can make the distinction, because it responds to the element $\ket{\rm alive}\bra{\rm dead}$ of the density matrix, and that element is fragile. A laboratory version replaces the cat by a microwave cavity field in a superposition of two coherent states of opposite phase, $\ket\beta+\ket{-\beta}$ up to normalization. The mean photon number is $\bar n=|\beta|^2$. Photons leak out at the energy decay rate $\kappa$. Leakage acts as a weak beam splitter between the cavity and the outside field, and a coherent state divides into two coherent states:"),
        ("eq", r"""\ket{\pm\beta}_{\rm cav}\ket0_{\rm out}\;\longrightarrow\;
\ket{\pm\beta e^{-\kappa t/2}}_{\rm cav}\,\ket{\pm\beta\sqrt{1-e^{-\kappa t}}}_{\rm out}."""),
        ("p", r"Each component leaves its own coherent state in the outside field. Their overlap, $\langle-\gamma|\gamma\rangle=e^{-2|\gamma|^2}$, multiplies the coherence between the components, as for any record left in an environment. With $|\gamma|^2=\bar n(1-e^{-\kappa t})$ and $\beta_t=\beta e^{-\kappa t/2}$, the weight $C$ of the coherence terms $\ket{\pm\beta_t}\bra{\mp\beta_t}$ relative to the population terms becomes"),
        ("eq", r"""C(t)=\exp\!\left[-2\bar n\left(1-e^{-\kappa t}\right)\right]
\approx e^{-2\bar n\kappa t}\quad(\kappa t\ll1)."""),
        ("p", r"Each component loses energy at rate $\kappa$, but the coherence between them disappears $2\bar n$ times faster. The rate grows with the squared separation $|2\beta|^2$ of the components in phase space. Coherence falls to $e^{-2}$ once a single photon, on average, has escaped, whatever the size of $\bar n$. Brune, Haroche and colleagues in Paris observed this progressive decoherence in 1996, using fields of a few photons probed by Rydberg atoms. Components further apart lost their coherence faster."),
        ("p", r"For a massive object the environment is usually scattered gas molecules or thermal radiation. In the quantum Brownian motion model, with relaxation rate $\gamma$ and temperature $T$, a superposition of two positions a distance $\Delta x$ apart loses coherence at the rate"),
        ("eq", r"""\tau_D^{-1}=\gamma\left(\frac{\Delta x}{\lambda_{\rm th}}\right)^2,\qquad
\lambda_{\rm th}=\frac{\hbar}{\sqrt{2mk_BT}}."""),
        ("p", r"For a mass of one gram at 300 K, $\lambda_{\rm th}\approx4\times10^{-23}$ m, and a separation of 1 cm gives $\tau_D\approx10^{-41}/\gamma$. The formula assumes high temperature and a separation shorter than the wavelength of the environmental particles. For larger separations each collision resolves the position, and the rate saturates at the collision rate. Either estimate puts decoherence far below any time on which the motion of the object could be observed."),
        ("p", r"Decoherence explains why the alive and dead components cannot be made to interfere, and why the record forms in macroscopic configurations. It does not state which component is found. The reduced density matrix of the cat becomes indistinguishable from a mixture, while the global state remains a superposition under unitary evolution. Whether a further physical process selects one outcome, as objective-collapse models propose, is the measurement problem. Interference with ever larger molecules and mechanical oscillators continues to constrain such models."),
    ],
    "spin_physics": [
        ("p", r"In 1922 Stern and Gerlach sent a beam of silver atoms through a strongly inhomogeneous magnetic field and collected them on a glass plate. Magnetic moments with random classical orientations would have spread the atoms into a continuous band. The deposit instead showed two separate lines. A silver atom has one unpaired 5s electron with no orbital angular momentum. In modern terms, the two lines reveal an angular momentum intrinsic to the electron, its spin."),
        ("p", r"Spin obeys the same commutation relations as orbital angular momentum, given under the angular momentum operator, but its quantum number can be half-integer. For the electron $s=1/2$, so its spin component along any axis takes the two values $\pm\hbar/2$. The spin produces a magnetic moment and a Zeeman energy:"),
        ("eq", r"""\boldsymbol\mu=-g_s\mu_B\frac{\mathbf S}{\hbar},\qquad U=-\boldsymbol\mu\cdot\mathbf B,
\qquad \mu_B=\frac{e\hbar}{2m_e}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $g_s\approx2.0023$ is the electron g-factor and $\mu_B$ the Bohr magneton. In a field whose strength changes along its own direction $z$, the force on the atom is $F_z=-g_sm_s\mu_B\,\partial B_z/\partial z$ with $m_s=\pm1/2$. The two signs push the atom up or down by equal amounts. For an atom of mass $M$ and speed $v$ crossing a magnet of length $L$, each beam is displaced by"),
        ("eq", r"""\Delta z=\frac{g_s\mu_B}{2}\left|\frac{\partial B_z}{\partial z}\right|\frac{L^2}{2Mv^2}."""),
        ("p", r"A gradient of $10^3$ T/m over 3.5 cm acting on silver atoms moving at 550 m/s gives 0.10 mm per beam. The two beams then lie about 0.2 mm apart, comparable to the separation recorded in 1922. The calculation keeps only the gradient along the strong field. Because $\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$, transverse gradients are also present, but the moment precesses rapidly about the strong field and their effect averages out."),
        ("p", r"The same Zeeman energy makes the spin precess about a uniform field at the Larmor frequency. An oscillating field at that frequency drives transitions, which is magnetic resonance:"),
        ("eq", r"""\omega_L=\frac{g_s\mu_BB}{\hbar},\qquad \frac{\omega_L}{2\pi B}\approx28.0\ \mathrm{GHz/T}."""),
        ("p", r"Electron spin resonance spectrometers working at 9.5 GHz therefore operate near 0.34 T. The rotation of the spin direction follows from the Pauli matrices, and a rotation by $2\pi$ changes the sign of the spinor, which neutron interferometers have measured. For protons, the nuclear magneton and the measured proton g-factor replace $\mu_B$ and $g_s$. Nuclear magnetic resonance accordingly works at hundreds of megahertz in fields of several tesla."),
        ("p", r"The value of $g_s$ and its small departure from 2 are treated under electron. Spin also fixes how identical particles behave under exchange. The spin-statistics theorem of relativistic quantum field theory, stated here without proof, makes states of identical particles with half-integer spin antisymmetric and those with integer spin symmetric. Antisymmetry gives the exclusion principle, which produces the shell structure counted under quantum number."),
        ("p", r"A composite particle follows the total spin of its constituents: an even number of fermions makes a boson and an odd number a fermion. A helium-4 atom contains six fermions, two protons, two neutrons and two electrons, and is a boson. Helium-3 lacks one neutron and is a fermion. The two liquids have nearly the same interactions but opposite statistics. Liquid helium-4 becomes superfluid near the temperature at which an ideal Bose gas of the same density would condense, as worked out under Bose--Einstein statistics. Liquid helium-3 stays normal down to about 1 mK at low pressure. A superfluid appears only after its atoms bind into Cooper pairs with total spin one."),
    ],
}

